\f0\fs24 \cf2 As soon as you enter the Dome Gallery, your eyes are captured by a sumptuous fabric: a crimson satin curtain, made in Milan toward the end of the 19th century. It\'92s not just a decorative element; it\'92s a textile manifesto, a statement of identity for the Bagatti Valsecchi family. At the center, the family crest shines, embroidered with extraordinary precision. It\'92s divided into four sections, each with a symbol that tells a piece of the family\'92s history. On the left, against a red background, a soldier with sword and shield speaks of the family\'92s warrior origins. Below, on a golden background, a crouching ox symbolizes hard work, farming, and peasant roots. \
On the right, a crowned black eagle on a golden background represents strength, nobility, and ambition. And beside it, two golden lilies on a blue background, a clear reference to elegance and heraldic tradition. But there\'92s more: it all comes from a black pencil drawing, preserved in the museum\'92s archive. Sharp lines, a decisive stroke: this is where the work first came to life. Every curve of the crest, every detail, was transferred from paper to fabric with impressive fidelity.\
The result? A masterpiece that blends art and storytelling, elegance and memory. A simple curtain, yes. But also a piece of identity sewn through time.\
